#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491228} :activity/id #uuid "0099e534-c8ef-5835-b3d9-b972918f1743" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557 :topic/id #uuid "fee59c68-0ad2-5141-b297-31cf8632cc46" :topic/math-academy-id 2167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd7515d-bc8c-5fbf-b8b7-be4ee29a026e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415110} {:answer/id #uuid "229bfab5-ba1b-52fe-b0f1-2051f81b3eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415111} {:answer/id #uuid "383d6df1-6a22-5c04-94da-26eb4b0bd68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415120} {:answer/id #uuid "59b52a2f-1933-53c4-9f7d-6b819b9dbb83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415122} {:answer/id #uuid "7818ba2a-afff-5ecd-8ff6-f5c7aa948efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415124} :answer-field/id #uuid "54a4eb9e-e422-55cf-a2cc-fe7a41adc569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4a26f8e-8621-57f0-b0fb-289f901931d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136954" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{3}{1}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "039fc048-1ead-5768-afc5-9d4af82f37c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} {:answer/id #uuid "13cbf72a-775b-5500-b4ce-fd7dee8abc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324150} {:answer/id #uuid "6058f5f8-765c-528f-b9e3-77d370e52e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324776} {:answer/id #uuid "df18dbbf-d1af-5d62-9ba8-07e3f6047abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325920} {:answer/id #uuid "df1f5f2f-9dda-564b-8601-3ba072795d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323994} :answer-field/id #uuid "58847018-c5c9-5b9a-9650-5b07e75b721b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d34cdae0-7b23-59fc-a8bc-f0d99444106f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40921" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ced1024-a004-5b60-b5cb-3377985ac90e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491266}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26168406-f1b0-5099-9e07-25cd625153b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415112} {:answer/id #uuid "28a4b0d8-24a1-5c50-8d0d-3d18a2ad1c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415113} {:answer/id #uuid "37a33051-e1b1-5298-99ef-d2c6766952fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415119} {:answer/id #uuid "571ff69e-5db2-5300-b1f0-1d3e4bea31c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415121} {:answer/id #uuid "d61a5d2e-e72e-5b6e-9e93-29e9d0122c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415131} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3cd328-26f2-53b0-bd30-ec73823e7080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abb758c7-4ff1-5d36-aa75-96f61271e2e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-137094" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $14a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ -28 & 56 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{14} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n14a+\\frac{b}{c}=14\\cdot \\frac{9}{14}+\\frac{2}{1}=11.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a71b792-bbea-515e-8ba1-f0cd6295bfe8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1299ade0-03c4-5049-abac-d1b905ea647d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368556} {:answer/id #uuid "de3e0276-368e-58fc-81ca-2ba8066692a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368557} {:answer/id #uuid "ce89137e-5a01-54bb-9780-320b4e37ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368558} {:answer/id #uuid "ce900d5f-43f0-5f77-adbc-98050b478ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368559} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8250d4-f373-5760-9fc7-2ed30b0938ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368560}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368557} :answer-field/id #uuid "1812231c-b731-57f0-8e96-cc3da0e4fe33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8912c2e3-5605-5590-8ee8-b25e93901622" :question/math-academy-id "q-42245" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+4c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\n From the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-3)+2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+4c=(-2)+(-3)+4\\cdot 0=-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c604cb99-8f2f-571b-8dc3-8a3b1b413bdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7360267e-e517-562f-a7ab-234dea8e16b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267158} {:answer/id #uuid "8a852bda-8769-55af-b974-271e085d782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267380} {:answer/id #uuid "abc3d198-0e91-5133-9e31-d766dcdd7bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267694} {:answer/id #uuid "df227e4e-6f3a-5099-aa04-1ab50365aa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268116} {:answer/id #uuid "f0784891-ea63-5109-abfd-c29f5a65f3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267158} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fef7bda-8cd4-5d78-b705-1883723f2fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7264a872-6f66-52f9-bc5f-7b2565f54aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-137096" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f75e5e8e-a87b-5720-97a7-18016502e31b"}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490499}]}
